## Problem : Check Permutation 

## Statemants : 

For a given two strings, 'str1' and 'str2', check whether they are a permutation of each other or not.

Permutations of each other
Two strings are said to be a permutation of each other when either of the string's characters can be rearranged so that it becomes identical to the other one.

Example: 
str1= "sinrtg" 
str2 = "string"

The character of the first string(str1) can be rearranged to form str2 and hence we can say that the given strings are a permutation of each other.


## Example :

Sample Input 1:
abcde
baedc
Sample Output 1:
true
Sample Input 2:
abc
cbd
Sample Output 2:
false


## Aproach : First Brute - Force 

First, check whether the lengths of both strings are the same. If they are different, return false because they cannot be permutations.
Create a boolean visited array to keep track of which characters of the second string have already been used.
Use a nested loop:
The outer loop takes each character from the first string.
The inner loop checks that character against every character of the second string.
If the characters are the same and the character in the second string has not been visited:
Mark that position as visited.
Set found = true.
Break the inner loop because the same character cannot be used again.
After the inner loop finishes, check the found variable:
If found == false, it means no matching unused character was found, so return false.
Continue this process for every character of the first string.
If all characters find a matching character, return true.

## code 


public class Solution {

	public static boolean isPermutation(String str1, String str2) {
		//Your code goes here

           if(str1.length() != str2.length()){
              return false;
           }
       

        boolean visited[] = new boolean[str2.length()];

        for(int i = 0; i < str1.length(); i++){
           boolean found = false;
           for(int j = 0; j < str2.length(); j++){
              if(str1.charAt(i) == str2.charAt(j) && !visited[j]){
                 visited[j] = true;
                 found = true;
                   break;
              }
           }
           if(!found){
              return false;
           }
        }

           return true;
	}

}

## Space and Time Complexity 

TC : O(n^2)
SC : O(n)

## Another approach : Optimal Approach 
## Optimal Approach — Frequency Array

1. First, check whether the lengths of both strings are equal.

   * If the lengths are different, they cannot be permutations, so return `false`.

2. Create an integer frequency array of size `256`.

   * This array stores the frequency of each character.

3. Traverse the first string:

   * For every character, increment its frequency in the array.

4. Traverse the second string:

   * For every character, decrement its frequency in the array.

5. While decrementing, check whether the frequency becomes negative.

   * If it becomes negative, the second string contains that character more times than the first string.
   * Therefore, return `false`.

6. If we finish traversing the second string without finding a negative frequency, both strings contain the same characters with the same frequency.

7. Return `true`.



